'use strict';
const lessons=[
{id:'basics',title:'Start with the trading desk',tag:'01 · FOUNDATIONS',body:['A share represents ownership in a company. An exchange brings buyers and sellers together. A trading symbol identifies the security; the exchange and contract details matter too.','Bid is the price a buyer is offering. Ask is the price a seller is requesting. Buying immediately generally crosses to the ask, while selling crosses to the bid. This gap is the spread. Last traded price (LTP) is a reference, not a guaranteed execution price.','In Paperlane, your cash and orders are virtual and prices are synthetic. Practise using the controls first. Profits here do not establish that a strategy will work in the real market.'],example:'If bid is ₹99.90 and ask is ₹100.10, buying then immediately selling 10 shares costs about ₹2 in spread, before charges.',question:'Which price does an immediate market buy normally use?',answers:['The ask','Your preferred price','Yesterday’s close'],correct:0,explain:'A market buy uses available selling prices. This simulator fills at its current ask; real orders can fill at multiple prices.'},
{id:'orders',title:'Choose and manage an order',tag:'02 · EXECUTION',body:['A market order prioritises execution at available prices. A limit order specifies the highest buy price or lowest sell price you accept; it may never fill.','A stop-limit order has two prices. The trigger activates the limit order, then the limit constrains execution. A gap can leave a triggered order unfilled. A stop is not a guaranteed loss cap.','An order is an instruction; a trade is a completed fill; a position is what remains open. Cancelling a pending order prevents future execution, but does not undo fills. To exit a long position, sell its remaining quantity. To close a short, buy it back.'],example:'A sell stop-limit with trigger ₹95 and limit ₹94 can remain unfilled if price gaps straight to ₹90.',question:'Does cancelling an unfilled order close an existing position?',answers:['Yes, always','No, the position needs an opposite-side fill','Only for options'],correct:1,explain:'Pending orders and existing positions are separate. Review the instrument, side and quantity when preparing an exit.'},
{id:'pnl',title:'Understand your result',tag:'03 · P&L',body:['For a long position, gross open P&L is quantity × (current price − average entry). For a short, price decreases help. Paperlane uses signed quantity, so the same formula works for both.','Net open P&L adds all open positions. Gains on one leg can offset losses on another. A flat combined number does not necessarily mean quotes stopped.','Closing books a result. Fees and spread reduce what you keep. Paperlane’s Trade report allocates entry and exit fees to each closed portion. Its booked result deducts all fill fees, including fees paid on entries that are still open.'],example:'Buy 10 at ₹100, sell at ₹102: gross profit ₹20. If allocated charges are ₹3, net profit is ₹17.',question:'Gross profit is ₹20 and allocated fees are ₹3. What is net profit?',answers:['₹23','₹20','₹17'],correct:2,explain:'Net profit subtracts costs. The app’s fees are illustrative, not a current broker quotation.'},
{id:'risk',title:'Write a plan before entering',tag:'04 · RISK & PROCESS',body:['Write down the reason for entering, what would invalidate that reason, your intended exit, and the maximum loss you are prepared to plan for. These are decisions to review, not predictions.','Position size changes the impact of a price move. A planned stop can help estimate risk, but gaps, slippage and liquidity can make actual losses larger. Margin is collateral, not the maximum amount you can lose.','Keep practice size consistent while learning. Record why you changed a plan. Judge the process separately from the outcome: a profitable trade can still break your rules, and a planned trade can lose.'],example:'For shares, 20 units × a planned ₹5 adverse move = ₹100 before costs and slippage. That estimate is not a guaranteed loss limit.',question:'Does the margin requirement tell you the maximum possible loss?',answers:['Yes','No','Only if the trade is profitable'],correct:1,explain:'Margin is a funding requirement. Risk depends on the position and market moves; losses can exceed margin.'},
{id:'options',title:'Read an option contract',tag:'05 · OPTIONS',body:['Calls (CE) and puts (PE) are different contracts. A call gives the buyer the right to buy the underlying at its strike; a put gives the right to sell, subject to contract terms. Expiry and lot size are essential identifiers. Not every stock has options.','The premium is the option price per unit. Premium × lot size × lots gives the premium amount before charges. ₹6,000 is not a universal minimum. Option value depends on the underlying, time, volatility and other factors.','Buying an option risks the premium and costs, with exercise and settlement obligations also needing attention. Writing options can create much larger losses; an uncovered short call has theoretically unlimited loss. Avoid treating a SELL button as an exit unless you already own the matching quantity.','Our snapshot contracts and simulated pricing do not reproduce real liquidity, physical delivery or expiry settlement. Learn those rules before any real transaction.'],example:'A premium of ₹40 with 125 units per lot costs ₹5,000 for one lot, before charges. The strike is not the price paid.',question:'What is the premium amount for ₹40 × 125 units × 2 lots?',answers:['₹80','₹5,000','₹10,000'],correct:2,explain:'Multiply premium per unit by units per lot and number of lots. Lot sizes vary across contracts.'},
{id:'review',title:'Review a trade without hindsight',tag:'06 · REFLECTION',body:['Start with facts: instrument, direction, size, entry, exit, holding time, charges and result. Then compare the recorded plan with what you did. Do not invent an entry reason after seeing the outcome.','Ask: Was the order type appropriate? Did I recognise the total quantity? Did I follow my planned exit? Was there a gap between expected and actual costs? Save one specific thing to practise next.','One trade or a high win rate alone cannot establish an edge. Losing trades can exceed many small gains. Synthetic fills cannot validate real execution or strategy profitability.','Local reviews in this app use fixed rules and calculated ledger values. They are not AI predictions or personalised trade recommendations. External AI is not connected, and no journal or trade data is sent to an AI provider.'],example:'“Practise cancelling an unfilled order” is a clearer next step than “be better at trading.”',question:'Can a winning paper trade prove a real-market strategy works?',answers:['Yes','No','Only if the profit is large'],correct:1,explain:'A single simulated result is not evidence of a reliable strategy. Review execution, costs and repeated behaviour.'},
{"id": "candles", "title": "Read candles and timeframes", "tag": "07 · CHART READING", "body": ["A candle summarises prices during one interval: open, high, low and close. Its body runs between open and close; its wicks show the extremes. A green candle means close exceeded open, not necessarily that price rose from the previous candle.", "A candle still forming can change until its interval ends. A single shape does not reveal the next move. Compare observations across several bars before writing an interpretation.", "In this app, choose 10s, 1m or 5m. Larger intervals combine more observed ticks into each candle. Missing history is not invented, so changing intervals may leave too few bars for an indicator."], "example": "Open ₹100, high ₹104, low ₹98, close ₹102: the body is ₹100–₹102 and the total range is ₹6.", "question": "What does a candle’s high minus low measure?", "answers": ["Its profit", "Its price range", "Its volume"], "correct": 1, "explain": "₹104 − ₹98 = ₹6. A candle range is not the profit of a trade."},
{"id": "levels", "title": "Mark support, resistance and trends", "tag": "08 · CHART READING", "body": ["Support and resistance describe areas where price previously paused or reversed. Treat them as zones to observe, not walls that price must respect. A trend can be described by a sequence of rising or falling highs and lows.", "Use Level to mark a price and Trend to connect two observed points. Choose anchors you could have identified at the time, rather than selecting only the ones that make a past trade look obvious.", "For practice, note what would contradict your interpretation. A move through a level can reverse again. Drawings are personal annotations; they do not place orders or enforce stops."], "example": "Price has turned near ₹100 several times. Mark that area and record whether later completed candles stay above or below it; do not label every touch a guaranteed buy.", "question": "Does a drawn support line guarantee a bounce?", "answers": ["Yes, after three touches", "No", "Only on a 5-minute chart"], "correct": 1, "explain": "A line records an observation. Future buying and selling can carry price through it.", "source": {"label": "CME: Support and resistance", "url": "https://www.cmegroup.com/education/courses/technical-analysis/support-and-resistance"}},
{"id": "averages", "title": "Use SMA and EMA thoughtfully", "tag": "09 · INDICATORS", "body": ["SMA averages closing prices over a chosen number of bars. EMA gives more weight to recent closes. Both summarise past prices and lag new moves; neither can promise the next direction.", "A shorter period responds sooner but can fluctuate more. A longer period smooths more history and reacts more slowly. A crossover can repeatedly reverse in a sideways market.", "Open Indicators, choose SMA or EMA, then set period, colour and width. Keep the candle interval unchanged while comparing periods. This chart needs a complete initial window before drawing either average."], "example": "For closes ₹100, ₹102 and ₹104, SMA 3 is ₹102. SMA 20 on 1-minute candles covers a different set of observations from SMA 20 on 5-minute candles.", "question": "What changes when you increase an SMA period?", "answers": ["It guarantees a better signal", "It uses more closing prices", "It starts predicting prices"], "correct": 1, "explain": "A larger window uses more history and usually smooths more, with greater lag.", "source": {"label": "Fidelity: Technical indicator guide", "url": "https://www.fidelity.com/learning-center/trading-investing/technical-analysis/technical-indicator-guide/overview?filter=Overlay"}},
{"id": "rsi", "title": "Read and customise RSI", "tag": "10 · INDICATORS", "body": ["RSI compares recent upward and downward price changes on a 0–100 scale. A common period is 14. A high reading can persist during an uptrend; a low reading can persist during a downtrend.", "The period changes the calculation. Reference levels such as 30/70 or 40/60 only move the horizontal guides. Moving a guide does not change RSI itself, and crossing a guide is not an automatic trade instruction.", "Choose Indicators → RSI to customise the period and levels. In this app RSI 14 needs at least 15 observed closes. If you chart an option, RSI describes that option’s premium, not the underlying stock."], "example": "With RSI at 55, changing the upper guide from 70 to 60 leaves the reading at 55. Changing the period from 14 to 40 can change the reading.", "question": "Which setting changes the RSI calculation?", "answers": ["The line colour", "The upper reference level", "The period"], "correct": 2, "explain": "The period changes the history used; reference levels and colours only change presentation.", "source": {"label": "Fidelity: Relative Strength Index", "url": "https://www.fidelity.com/learning-center/trading-investing/technical-analysis/technical-indicator-guide/rsi"}},
{"id": "sizing", "title": "Translate a risk budget into quantity", "tag": "11 · RISK PRACTICE", "body": ["For a simple long share example, planned loss per unit is entry minus planned stop price. Divide a chosen practice loss budget by that distance, then round quantity down to valid units. Include a buffer for costs.", "This arithmetic is only a planning estimate. A stop may fill worse than expected or a stop-limit may not fill. Available funds and contract lot sizes also constrain quantity.", "If the smallest permitted quantity exceeds your planned budget, skipping the trade is a valid decision. Do not move the stop closer merely to make an unaffordable quantity appear acceptable."], "example": "Practice budget ₹200, entry ₹100, planned stop ₹96: ₹200 ÷ ₹4 = 50 shares before allowing for costs. With a ₹20 cost buffer, round ₹180 ÷ ₹4 down to 45 shares.", "question": "With ₹180 available for price risk and ₹4 risk per share, what quantity fits?", "answers": ["45 shares", "180 shares", "720 shares"], "correct": 0, "explain": "180 ÷ 4 = 45. This remains a planned estimate, not a guaranteed maximum loss."},
{"id": "expectancy", "title": "Look beyond the win rate", "tag": "12 · PERFORMANCE", "body": ["Win rate is the fraction of trades that win. It says nothing by itself about the size of gains, losses or costs. Use net results consistently when comparing your own records.", "A sample average result is total net P&L divided by the number of closed trades or portions you counted. Define that unit consistently: an app’s partial close is not always a separate full round trip.", "Compare average gain, average loss and the largest losing run alongside win rate. A small or selectively chosen sample can mislead; synthetic results are practice evidence, not proof of real execution quality."], "example": "Six wins of ₹100 and four losses of ₹200 produce ₹600 − ₹800 = −₹200 before costs, despite a 60% win rate.", "question": "Is a 60% win rate enough to establish profitability?", "answers": ["Yes", "No, gain/loss sizes and costs matter", "Only for options"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Frequent small wins can be outweighed by fewer larger losses."},
{"id": "liquidity", "title": "Understand spreads and slippage", "tag": "13 · EXECUTION", "body": ["A spread is the gap between bid and ask. A wider spread increases the cost of entering and immediately exiting. A displayed quote may have limited quantity available.", "Slippage is the difference between an expected execution price and the actual fill. Fast movement and limited liquidity can cause a real market order to fill at several prices. A limit controls the acceptable price but does not guarantee a fill.", "Paperlane uses illustrative fills and does not model order-book depth or partial fills. Record expected versus actual entry and exit prices in practice, while recognising that real execution may be harder."], "example": "Bid ₹99, ask ₹101: buying 10 at the ask and immediately selling at the bid loses ₹20 before fees, even if the quoted midpoint stays ₹100.", "question": "What does a limit buy at ₹100 control?", "answers": ["It guarantees an immediate fill", "The maximum acceptable price per unit", "The future selling price"], "correct": 1, "explain": "A buy limit restricts execution price; it can remain unfilled."},
{"id": "moneyness", "title": "Separate strike, premium and break-even", "tag": "14 · OPTIONS", "body": ["Strike is the contract’s reference price; premium is what the option trades for. For a call, intrinsic value is the greater of underlying price minus strike or zero. For a put, reverse that subtraction.", "In-the-money means positive intrinsic value. At-the-money is near the underlying price, and out-of-the-money means no intrinsic value. None of these labels alone says whether buying the contract will be profitable.", "For a purchased call, expiry break-even before costs is strike plus premium paid; for a purchased put it is strike minus premium paid. Before expiry, time value and volatility also affect the sale price. These payoff examples exclude settlement obligations; this app does not simulate expiry settlement."], "example": "Underlying ₹105, call strike ₹100, premium ₹8: intrinsic value is ₹5 and the remaining ₹3 is extrinsic value. Expiry break-even before costs is ₹108.", "question": "What is the call’s expiry break-even for strike ₹100 and premium ₹8?", "answers": ["₹100", "₹92", "₹108"], "correct": 2, "explain": "Strike plus premium is ₹108 before costs. An in-the-money call can still lose relative to the premium paid."},
{"id": "greeks", "title": "Why an option can lose despite the right direction", "tag": "15 · OPTIONS", "body": ["Option premiums respond to several inputs together. Delta estimates sensitivity to a small underlying price change; gamma describes how delta changes. These are local estimates, not fixed promises.", "Theta describes sensitivity to time passing, while vega describes sensitivity to implied volatility. A purchased option can lose value as time passes or implied volatility falls, even when the underlying moves in the expected direction.", "Do not infer the cause of an actual loss from direction alone. Compare recorded premium prices and costs first; without historical volatility and other inputs, an explanation about time decay or volatility is only a possibility. This app does not show a live Greeks feed."], "example": "A call bought for ₹50 is later sold for ₹45 while the stock rose slightly. The realised premium loss is ₹5 per unit before costs; that price observation alone does not tell you which Greek dominated.", "question": "Can a purchased call fall in value while its underlying rises?", "answers": ["Yes, other pricing inputs also matter", "No, never", "Only if the strike changes"], "correct": 0, "explain": "Time and implied volatility can offset the effect of a favourable underlying move.", "source": {"label": "Zerodha: Option Greeks", "url": "https://support.zerodha.com/category/account-opening/glossary/futures-and-options/articles/what-are-option-greeks"}},
{"id": "routine", "title": "Build a repeatable practice routine", "tag": "16 · HABITS", "body": ["Before a practice trade, check instrument, exchange, expiry where relevant, side, quantity, order type and total amount. Write the idea and intended exit before seeing the outcome.", "After a loss, pause before increasing size or immediately trying to win it back. Record whether the next decision follows your plan or is a reaction to the previous result. A no-trade session can still be productive practice.", "At the end of a session, review one decision that followed your plan and one to improve. Change one practice habit at a time so you can compare sessions. Course completion is a learning milestone, not certification or evidence that you are ready to risk real money."], "example": "A useful session goal is “verify side and quantity before every order,” rather than “make ₹1,000 today.” The first is a behaviour you can control.", "question": "Which is a process goal?", "answers": ["Recover yesterday’s loss today", "Double size after every loss", "Check side and quantity before submission"], "correct": 2, "explain": "A process goal describes an action under your control, independent of market outcomes."},
{"id": "futures", "title": "Futures and options are different", "tag": "17 · F&O BASICS", "body": ["A future is an agreement to buy or sell an underlying asset at a set price on a future date. Both sides have obligations. An option gives its buyer a right, while the seller takes on an obligation.", "F&O contracts have an expiry date and a lot size. A small amount of money can control a much larger exposure. This can multiply losses as well as gains.", "Paperlane currently lets you practise option orders. It does not model futures, contract settlement or real exchange margin. Use these lessons to understand the language before trading either product with real money."], "example": "A future on 100 units changes by ₹500 when its price changes ₹5 per unit. Margin paid is not the maximum loss.", "question": "Which side of an option has an obligation if the contract is exercised?", "answers": ["The buyer", "The seller", "Neither side"], "correct": 1, "explain": "The option buyer has a right; the option seller takes the matching obligation.", "source": {"label": "SEBI: Understanding derivatives", "url": "https://investor.sebi.gov.in/understanding_derivatives.html"}},
{"id": "option_side", "title": "Call, put, buy and sell", "tag": "18 · F&O BASICS", "body": ["A call (CE) gives its buyer the right to buy the underlying at its strike. A put (PE) gives its buyer the right to sell at its strike. The option seller, also called the writer, takes the other side.", "BUY CE and BUY PE both pay a premium. SELL CE and SELL PE receive a premium but can face much larger losses. A SELL order also closes an option you already bought if it matches that position and quantity.", "Always check whether SELL is closing your long position or opening a new short position. In Paperlane, the order ticket can open a short when you sell more than you own."], "example": "You own 1 lot of a CE. Selling that same lot closes it. Selling 2 lots closes 1 lot and opens a short of 1 lot.", "question": "You own one CE lot and press SELL for two lots. What can happen?", "answers": ["Only the long closes", "One lot closes and one short opens", "Nothing"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Selling more than you own can create a short option position.", "source": {"label": "SEBI: Understanding derivatives", "url": "https://investor.sebi.gov.in/understanding_derivatives.html"}},
{"id": "option_chain", "title": "Read an option chain carefully", "tag": "19 · F&O BASICS", "body": ["First choose the underlying and expiry. Each row is a strike price. CE quotes are call premiums and PE quotes are put premiums. Bid is what a buyer offers; ask is what a seller wants.", "The lot size tells you how many units one contract contains. Premium × lot size × number of lots gives the cash premium amount before charges. A low premium does not mean low risk if you are selling an option.", "The chain in Paperlane uses saved contract metadata and synthetic premiums. It does not contain live bid depth, open interest or actual market liquidity. An option listed in the catalogue is not automatically a good trade."], "example": "Premium ₹40, lot size 125, two lots: ₹40 × 125 × 2 = ₹10,000 before charges.", "question": "What is the premium amount for ₹40 and 125 units in one lot?", "answers": ["₹40", "₹5,000", "₹125"], "correct": 1, "explain": "₹40 × 125 = ₹5,000 before charges.", "source": {"label": "Zerodha: Option premium", "url": "https://support.zerodha.com/category/account-opening/glossary/futures-and-options/articles/what-is-option-premium"}},
{"id": "option_payoff", "title": "Premium, expiry payoff and break-even", "tag": "20 · F&O BASICS", "body": ["At expiry, a call is worth the amount by which the underlying is above its strike, or zero. A put is worth the amount by which the underlying is below its strike, or zero.", "For a bought option, subtract the premium paid to find the simple expiry result per unit. For a written option, the sign is reversed. Multiply by lot size and lots. Real charges and settlement can change the outcome.", "Bought call break-even at expiry is strike plus premium. Bought put break-even is strike minus premium. Before expiry, the option can be sold at a price that also reflects remaining time and implied volatility."], "example": "Buy a 100 CE for ₹8. If the underlying ends at ₹105, expiry value is ₹5 and the result is a ₹3 loss per unit before charges. Break-even is ₹108.", "question": "A bought call has strike ₹100 and premium ₹8. Its expiry break-even before charges is:", "answers": ["₹92", "₹100", "₹108"], "correct": 2, "explain": "₹100 + ₹8 = ₹108. Being in the money at ₹105 still leaves a loss.", "source": {"label": "Zerodha: Option premium", "url": "https://support.zerodha.com/category/account-opening/glossary/futures-and-options/articles/what-is-option-premium"}},
{"id": "option_risks", "title": "Time, volatility and selling risk", "tag": "21 · F&O BASICS", "body": ["Time to expiry and expected volatility affect option premiums. Theta describes sensitivity to passing time; vega describes sensitivity to implied volatility. Delta estimates sensitivity to a small underlying price move. These values change and are not promises.", "An option buyer can lose the premium paid and costs. An uncovered option seller can lose much more than the premium received; an uncovered short call has theoretically unlimited upside risk.", "Margin is collateral required to carry a position, not a cap on loss. Paperlane uses a simple practice margin rule; it does not reproduce exchange or broker requirements. The app has no live Greeks feed."], "example": "A call can lose premium even if the stock rises slightly, because time passed or implied volatility fell. You cannot identify the exact cause without more data.", "question": "Does the margin held for a short option show its maximum loss?", "answers": ["Yes", "No", "Only for calls"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Margin is collateral. Loss can be much larger, especially on an uncovered short call.", "source": {"label": "Zerodha: Option Greeks", "url": "https://support.zerodha.com/category/account-opening/glossary/futures-and-options/articles/what-are-option-greeks"}},
{"id": "option_execution", "title": "Costs, liquidity and expiry", "tag": "22 · F&O BASICS", "body": ["An option can show a profit on a chart yet be hard to exit at that price. Wide bid-ask spreads, thin order books, fast movement and charges all affect the actual result.", "Expiry rules differ between index and stock derivatives, and settlement can create obligations. Check the current exchange and broker rules for the exact contract before any real trade.", "Paperlane does not simulate partial fills, real order-book depth or expiry settlement. Its chain prices and fills are synthetic. Use the paper account to practise the workflow, not to infer real-world strategy returns."], "example": "Bid ₹19 and ask ₹21: buying at the ask and selling immediately at the bid loses ₹2 per unit before fees, even if the quoted midpoint stays ₹20.", "question": "Which price does an immediate sell normally meet?", "answers": ["The ask", "The bid", "The strike"], "correct": 1, "explain": "An immediate sell generally meets available buying quotes, starting at the bid. Actual fills can vary.", "source": {"label": "NSE: Equity derivatives settlement", "url": "https://www.nseindia.com/static/products-services/equity-derivatives-settlement-mechanism"}}
];
const exercises=[{id:'limit',title:'Place a limit order',steps:'Choose a stock, select LIMIT, enter one valid lot and a buy price below the ask. Submit only if you intend to practise. Check whether it is pending or filled.',target:'orders'}, {id:'cancel',title:'Cancel a pending order',steps:'Open Orders, find a pending limit order and cancel it. Confirm its status and that reserved funds are released. A fill that already occurred cannot be cancelled.',target:'orders'}, {id:'roundtrip',title:'Complete a small round trip',steps:'Buy a small virtual position, then use Prepare exit. Review the opposite side and exact quantity before submitting. Find the resulting closed portion in Trade report.',target:'report'}, {id:'journal',title:'Reflect on one decision',steps:'Write your entry reason, planned exit and one lesson in Trading journal. Describe what happened without assuming that a good result means a good decision.',target:'journal'},{"id": "candles", "title": "Compare two candle intervals", "steps": "Open Terminal and compare 10s with 1m for the same instrument. Note how the candle shapes and number of observed bars change. No order is needed.", "target": "terminal"},{"id": "indicator", "title": "Customise and compare an indicator", "steps": "In Terminal, add SMA 10, then edit it to SMA 40. Keep the timeframe fixed. Note the difference in responsiveness; wait for enough observed bars if necessary.", "target": "terminal"},{"id": "levels", "title": "Mark a level before the next move", "steps": "In Terminal, use Level to mark a prior turning area. Write in your journal what would contradict your interpretation, then observe new candles without moving the original line.", "target": "terminal"},{"id": "costs", "title": "Explain a result after costs", "steps": "Open Trade report. Pick a closed portion, calculate gross price change × quantity, then subtract its allocated charges. Compare with the displayed net result.", "target": "report"}];
let learningData=null,learningPage='course',lessonIndex=0,learningLoading=false;
function learningTabs(){return `<div class="learn-nav">${[['course','Learn'],['fno','F&O lab'],['practice','Practise'],['plans','Trade plans'],['reviews','Review']].map(([id,label])=>`<button class="quiet ${learningPage===id?'chosen':''}" data-learning-page="${id}">${label}</button>`).join('')}</div>`;}
async function showLearning(){if($('learning-panel'))return;$('activity-content').innerHTML='<div id="learning-panel" class="learning-panel"><p class="empty">Loading your learning workspace…</p></div>';await refreshLearning();}
async function refreshLearning(){if(learningLoading)return;learningLoading=true;try{learningData=await api('/api/learning');paintLearning();}catch(e){if($('learning-panel'))$('learning-panel').innerHTML=`<p class="empty">${escapeHtml(e.message)}</p><button class="quiet" data-learning-retry>Retry learning workspace</button>`;}finally{learningLoading=false;}}
function paintLearning(){if(!$('learning-panel')||!learningData)return;const completed=learningData.completed||[],done=learningData.exercises||[];
const linkedCount=typeof reportData!=='undefined'&&reportData?.closed?reportData.closed.filter(t=>t.plan).length:null;
const heading=`<div class="learn-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">LEARN → PRACTISE → REVIEW</p><h2>Build your trading foundations.</h2><p>Small lessons. Deliberate practice. Honest reflection.</p></div><div class="learning-progress"><strong>${completed.length} / ${lessons.length}</strong><span>lessons completed</span><progress max="${lessons.length}" value="${completed.length}" aria-label="Lesson completion"></progress></div></div><div class="learning-actual-progress"><span><b>${completed.length}</b> lessons completed</span><span><b>${done.length}</b> practice exercises self-checked</span><span><b>${(learningData.plans||[]).length}</b> plans saved</span>${linkedCount===null?'':`<span><b>${linkedCount}</b> closed portions with a linked plan</span>`}</div>${learningTabs()}`;
let body='';if(learningPage==='course'){const l=lessons[lessonIndex];body=`<div class="course-layout"><aside class="course-list">${lessons.map((x,index)=>`<button data-lesson="${index}" class="${index===lessonIndex?'current':''}"><small>${x.tag}</small><b>${escapeHtml(x.title)}</b><span>${completed.includes(x.id)?'✓ Completed':'Read & check understanding'}</span></button>`).join('')}</aside><article class="lesson"><p class="eyebrow">${l.tag}</p><h3>${escapeHtml(l.title)}</h3>${l.body.map(p=>`<p>${escapeHtml(p)}</p>`).join('')}<div class="lesson-example"><b>Worked example</b><p>${escapeHtml(l.example)}</p></div>${l.source?`<p class="learning-sources">Read more: <a href="${l.source.url}" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">${escapeHtml(l.source.label)}</a></p>`:''}<form id="lesson-quiz"><fieldset><legend>${escapeHtml(l.question)}</legend>${l.answers.map((a,n)=>`<label><input type="radio" name="lesson-answer" value="${n}" required> ${escapeHtml(a)}</label>`).join('')}</fieldset><button class="primary" type="submit">Check my understanding</button><p id="quiz-feedback" role="status"></p></form><button class="quiet" data-next-lesson>${lessonIndex===lessons.length-1?'Go to guided practice':'Next lesson →'}</button></article></div>`;}
if(learningPage==='practice')body=`<div class="learn-disclaimer">Exercises use your current paper account. Buttons open the relevant screen; they never place or cancel orders automatically. Completion is your self-check, not an independently verified score.</div><div class="exercise-grid">${exercises.map(e=>`<article><span class="tiny">${done.includes(e.id)?'✓ SELF-CHECKED':'GUIDED PRACTICE'}</span><h3>${e.title}</h3><p>${e.steps}</p><button class="quiet" data-practice-open="${e.target}">Open ${e.target==='report'?'trade report':e.target}</button><button class="mini-btn" data-exercise-done="${e.id}" ${done.includes(e.id)?'disabled':''}>I completed this</button></article>`).join('')}</div>`;

if(learningPage==='fno')body=`<div class="learn-disclaimer"><b>F&O practice lab</b><p>Start with lessons 17–22, then change the numbers below. This shows only a simple result at expiry. It is not a quote, forecast, broker margin estimate or settlement calculator.</p></div><div class="fno-lab"><div class="fno-lab-form"><h3>Explore an option at expiry</h3><label>Contract<select id="fno-kind"><option value="call">Call (CE)</option><option value="put">Put (PE)</option></select></label><label>Your side<select id="fno-side"><option value="buy">Buy the option</option><option value="sell">Sell / write the option</option></select></label><label>Strike price (₹)<input id="fno-strike" type="number" min="1" step="0.01" value="100" inputmode="decimal"></label><label>Premium per unit (₹)<input id="fno-premium" type="number" min="0.01" step="0.01" value="8" inputmode="decimal"></label><label>Units per lot<input id="fno-lot" type="number" min="1" step="1" value="50" inputmode="numeric"></label><label>Number of lots<input id="fno-lots" type="number" min="1" step="1" value="1" inputmode="numeric"></label><label>Underlying at expiry (₹)<input id="fno-spot" type="number" min="0" step="0.01" value="105" inputmode="decimal"></label></div><div id="fno-results" class="fno-lab-results" aria-live="polite"></div></div><div class="fno-checks"><h3>Check your understanding</h3><p>Try these without placing an order.</p><article><p><b>1.</b> You buy one 100 CE for ₹8. The underlying ends at ₹105. Is it a profit?</p><button class="quiet" data-fno-answer="call-loss">Show answer</button><p class="fno-answer" hidden>It loses ₹3 per unit before charges. The option is worth ₹5 at expiry, less than the ₹8 paid.</p></article><article><p><b>2.</b> You sell an option and receive ₹500 premium. Is ₹500 your maximum possible loss?</p><button class="quiet" data-fno-answer="short-risk">Show answer</button><p class="fno-answer" hidden>No. The premium received is the most you can keep if the option expires worthless. Losses can be much larger.</p></article><article><p><b>3.</b> Your purchased option rises from ₹20 to ₹22, but the bid is ₹19. What matters for an immediate exit?</p><button class="quiet" data-fno-answer="bid">Show answer</button><p class="fno-answer" hidden>The available bid matters. A visible last price or midpoint is not a guaranteed selling price.</p></article></div><p class="learning-sources">Learn more: <a href="https://investor.sebi.gov.in/understanding_derivatives.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SEBI derivatives guide</a> · <a href="https://support.zerodha.com/category/account-opening/glossary/futures-and-options/articles/what-is-option-premium" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Option premium explained</a>.</p>`;
if(learningPage==='plans')body=`<div class="learn-disclaimer">Record a plan before placing an order. This note does not create an order or enforce a stop-loss. Only a plan you explicitly select on an opening order is linked to that fill. Older fills have no recorded plan.</div><form id="trade-plan-form" class="plan-form"><label>Instrument<select name="symbol" required>${(state?.instruments||[]).filter(i=>i.kind!=='INDEX').map(i=>`<option value="${escapeHtml(i.symbol)}">${escapeHtml(instrumentLabel(i))}</option>`).join('')}</select></label><label>Why am I entering?<textarea name="reason" required maxlength="2000" placeholder="What is the idea, and what would invalidate it?"></textarea></label><label>Planned exit<textarea name="exit_plan" required maxlength="2000" placeholder="When and why will I exit?"></textarea></label><div class="form-row"><label>Maximum planned loss (₹)<input name="max_loss" type="number" min="0.01" max="100000000" step="0.01" required></label><label>Confidence<select name="confidence">${[1,2,3,4,5].map(n=>`<option value="${n}">${n} / 5</option>`).join('')}</select></label></div><button class="primary">Save plan locally</button><p id="plan-feedback" role="status"></p></form><div class="saved-plans">${(learningData.plans||[]).map(p=>`<article><b>${escapeHtml(symbolLabel(p.symbol))}</b><small>${stamp(p.created_at)} · Planned loss ${money(p.max_loss)} · Confidence ${p.confidence}/5</small><p>${escapeHtml(p.reason)}</p><p><strong>Exit:</strong> ${escapeHtml(p.exit_plan)}</p></article>`).join('')||'<p class="empty">No plans saved yet.</p>'}</div>`;
if(learningPage==='reviews')body='<div class="learn-disclaimer"><b>Local rule-based review · AI not connected</b><p>Uses calculated ledger facts. No trade or journal data is sent externally. It cannot judge your motivation, verify plan adherence, predict markets or validate profitability with synthetic prices.</p></div><button class="quiet" data-load-reviews>Refresh closed trades</button><div id="learning-reviews"><p class="empty">Loading closed trades…</p></div>';
$('learning-panel').innerHTML=heading+body+`<p class="learning-sources">Further learning: <a href="https://www.nseindia.com/static/invest/be-a-smart-investor" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NSE investor guidance</a> · <a href="https://investor.sebi.gov.in/iematerial.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SEBI education library</a>. This introductory course is not a certification or a trade recommendation.</p>`;
if(learningPage==='reviews')loadLearningReviews();if(learningPage==='fno')paintFnoPayoff();}
function expiryPayoff(kind,side,strike,premium,lot,lots,spot){
 const values=[strike,premium,lot,lots,spot];if(values.some(v=>!Number.isFinite(v))||strike<=0||premium<=0||spot<0||!Number.isInteger(lot)||lot<1||!Number.isInteger(lots)||lots<1||!['call','put'].includes(kind)||!['buy','sell'].includes(side))return null;
 const intrinsic=kind==='call'?Math.max(spot-strike,0):Math.max(strike-spot,0);
 const unit=(intrinsic-premium)*(side==='buy'?1:-1);
 return {intrinsic,unit,total:unit*lot*lots,breakeven:kind==='call'?strike+premium:(premium<=strike?strike-premium:null),premiumAmount:premium*lot*lots};
}
function paintFnoPayoff(){const host=$('fno-results');if(!host)return;const value=id=>Number($(id).value),kind=$('fno-kind').value,side=$('fno-side').value,strike=value('fno-strike'),premium=value('fno-premium'),lot=value('fno-lot'),lots=value('fno-lots'),spot=value('fno-spot');const result=expiryPayoff(kind,side,strike,premium,lot,lots,spot);if(!result||[strike,premium,lot,lots,spot].some(v=>v>100000000)){host.innerHTML='<p class="empty">Enter valid positive prices and whole-number lots to see the example.</p>';return;}const samples=[.8,.9,1,1.1,1.2].map(multiplier=>{const sampleSpot=Math.max(0,Math.round(strike*multiplier*100)/100),x=expiryPayoff(kind,side,strike,premium,lot,lots,sampleSpot);return `<tr><td>${money(sampleSpot)}</td><td>${money(x.intrinsic)}</td><td class="${color(x.total)}">${money(x.total)}</td></tr>`}).join('');host.innerHTML=`<p class="eyebrow">ILLUSTRATIVE RESULT AT EXPIRY</p><strong class="fno-result ${color(result.total)}">${money(result.total)}</strong><p>${side==='buy'?'You pay':'You receive'} ${money(result.premiumAmount)} in premium before charges. At the chosen expiry price, the option is worth ${money(result.intrinsic)} per unit.</p><p>Break-even at expiry before charges: <b>${result.breakeven===null?'No nonnegative expiry break-even':money(result.breakeven)}</b>. ${side==='buy'?'The premium paid is the most the option itself can lose before costs and settlement obligations.':kind==='call'?'An uncovered short call has no fixed maximum loss.':'A short put can lose much more than the premium received.'}</p><table><thead><tr><th>Underlying at expiry</th><th>Option value / unit</th><th>Result before costs</th></tr></thead><tbody>${samples}</tbody></table><p class="muted">This table ignores charges, tax, margin, time value, volatility, liquidity, exercise and delivery obligations. Paperlane does not simulate expiry settlement.</p>`;}
async function loadLearningReviews(){const host=$('learning-reviews');if(!host)return;try{const report=await api('/api/report');if(host!==$('learning-reviews'))return;host.innerHTML=report.closed.length?report.closed.map((t,index)=>`<article class="review-item"><div><b>${escapeHtml(symbolLabel(t.symbol))} · ${t.direction}</b><small>${stamp(t.exit_at)} · ${t.quantity} units</small></div><strong class="${color(t.net_pnl)}">${money(t.net_pnl)}</strong><button class="quiet" data-review-index="${index}">Review this trade</button></article>`).join(''):'<p class="empty">Close a paper position to unlock its review.</p>';host._report=report;}catch(e){host.innerHTML=`<p class="empty">${escapeHtml(e.message)}. Use Refresh closed trades to retry.</p>`;}}
function reviewFacts(t){const favourable=t.gross_pnl>=0;return [ `${t.direction==='LONG'?'Bought then sold':'Sold short then bought back'} ${t.quantity} units. Average entry ${money(t.entry_price)}; exit ${money(t.exit_price)}.`, `Gross result ${money(t.gross_pnl)}, allocated charges ${money(t.fees)}, net result ${money(t.net_pnl)}.`,favourable&&t.net_pnl<0?'The favourable gross result was smaller than costs. Review spread and fees when planning small targets.':t.net_pnl<0?'This closed portion lost money after costs. Compare the actual exit against your recorded plan; the result alone does not prove poor execution.':'This closed portion was profitable after costs. That does not establish a repeatable edge or prove you followed your plan.', 'A recorded plan may be linked to this fill. Compare it with what happened; the app cannot judge your motivation or whether you followed it.'];}
function technicalSnapshot(label,snapshot){
  const x=snapshot;
  if(!x||x.status==='unavailable')return `<article class="technical-card"><h4>${label}</h4><p>No indicator snapshot was recorded for this fill. Current chart readings cannot tell us what RSI was at that time.</p></article>`;
  const metric=(v,digits=2)=>Number.isFinite(v)?v.toFixed(digits):'Not enough bars';
  const messages=[];
  if(Number.isFinite(x.rsi14))messages.push(x.rsi14>=70?'RSI was in the commonly used upper zone (70+). This reflects strong recent upward momentum, not a guaranteed reversal or an automatic sell.':x.rsi14<=30?'RSI was in the commonly used lower zone (30 or below). This reflects strong recent downward momentum, not a guaranteed rebound or an automatic buy.':'RSI was between the common 30/70 thresholds. This alone does not identify a good entry or exit.');
  if(Number.isFinite(x.last_close)&&Number.isFinite(x.ema20))messages.push(`The last completed close was ${x.last_close>x.ema20?'above':x.last_close<x.ema20?'below':'equal to'} EMA 20. That is a lagging trend observation, not evidence that it caused this result.`);
  if(x.freshness!=='fresh')messages.push('The recorded sample was stale or missing. Do not treat it as a current signal at execution.');
  return `<article class="technical-card"><h4>${label}</h4><dl><div><dt>RSI 14</dt><dd>${metric(x.rsi14)}</dd></div><div><dt>SMA 10</dt><dd>${metric(x.sma10)}</dd></div><div><dt>EMA 20</dt><dd>${metric(x.ema20)}</dd></div></dl><small>As of ${stamp(x.as_of)} · ${x.contiguous_bar_count||0} consecutive completed 10s bars · ${escapeHtml(x.freshness||'unknown')} sample</small>${messages.map(p=>`<p>${escapeHtml(p)}</p>`).join('')}<p class="muted">${escapeHtml(Array.isArray(x.notes)?x.notes.join(' '):x.notes||'')}</p></article>`;
}
let currentReviewTrade=null;
function openTradeReview(t){
  currentReviewTrade=t;
  let dialog=$('trade-review-dialog');if(!dialog){dialog=document.createElement('dialog');dialog.id='trade-review-dialog';document.body.appendChild(dialog);}
  const explanation=PaperlaneReview.describe(t);
  dialog.innerHTML=`<div class="review-dialog-body"><button class="quiet dialog-close" data-close-review aria-label="Close trade review">Close ×</button><p class="eyebrow">LOCAL RULE-BASED REVIEW · NOT AI</p><h2>${escapeHtml(symbolLabel(t.symbol))}</h2><section class="review-cause"><h3>Why this trade ${t.net_pnl>0?'made a profit':t.net_pnl<0?'made a loss':'broke even'}</h3><p>${escapeHtml(explanation.reason)}</p><p>Entry ${money(t.entry_price)} → exit ${money(t.exit_price)}. Your ${t.direction.toLowerCase()} position ${explanation.perUnit>=0?'gained':'lost'} ${money(Math.abs(explanation.perUnit))} per unit × ${t.quantity} units = <b>${money(t.gross_pnl)}</b> gross.</p><p>After ${money(t.fees)} in allocated charges, the net result was <strong class="${color(t.net_pnl)}">${money(t.net_pnl)}</strong>.</p></section><section class="review-plan"><h3>Your plan and what happened</h3>${t.plan_link_status==='recorded'&&t.plan?`<p><b>Recorded reason:</b> ${escapeHtml(t.plan.reason)}</p><p><b>Planned exit:</b> ${escapeHtml(t.plan.exit_plan)}</p><p><b>Planned loss amount:</b> ${money(t.plan.max_loss)}. This was your plan, not a guaranteed loss cap.</p><p><b>Actual exit:</b> ${stamp(t.exit_at)} at ${money(t.exit_price)}, with a net result of ${money(t.net_pnl)}.</p><p>Compare these facts with your notes. The app cannot tell whether you followed the plan or why you chose to exit.</p>`:t.plan_link_status==='ambiguous'?'<p>This closed portion includes more than one opening fill with different plan links. We cannot assign one saved plan to the whole portion.</p>':'<p>No plan was linked to the opening fill. Older trades cannot be matched to a plan automatically.</p>'}</section><h3>What to improve or check</h3><ul class="review-actions">${explanation.improvements.map(p=>`<li>${escapeHtml(p)}</li>`).join('')}</ul><h3>Indicator context at the time</h3><p class="muted">Saved synthetic prices for this instrument, using completed 10-second bars only. If this is an option, these are premium indicators, not underlying-stock indicators. Entry refers to the initial opening fill; later additions change average entry cost.</p><div class="technical-grid">${technicalSnapshot('At entry',t.technical?.entry)}${technicalSnapshot('At exit',t.technical?.exit)}</div><p class="muted">Indicators describe past momentum and trend; they cannot prove why a trade won or lost. Missing data is not reconstructed. A recorded plan lets you compare your intention with the fill, but the app cannot judge plan adherence or motivation.</p><p class="learning-sources">Learn the indicators: <a href="https://www.fidelity.com/learning-center/trading-investing/technical-analysis/technical-indicator-guide/RSI" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">RSI</a> · <a href="https://www.fidelity.com/learning-center/trading-investing/technical-analysis/technical-indicator-guide/ema" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">EMA</a>. Synthetic results do not establish real-market strategy performance.</p><button class="quiet" data-review-chart>Show on chart</button> <button class="primary" data-review-journal>Reflect in my journal</button></div>`;
  dialog.showModal();
}

document.addEventListener('click',async event=>{const target=event.target.closest('button');if(!target)return;try{if(target.dataset.learningPage){learningPage=target.dataset.learningPage;paintLearning();}if(target.dataset.fnoAnswer){const answer=target.nextElementSibling;answer.hidden=!answer.hidden;target.textContent=answer.hidden?'Show answer':'Hide answer';}if(target.dataset.lesson!==undefined){lessonIndex=Number(target.dataset.lesson);paintLearning();}if(target.hasAttribute('data-next-lesson')){if(lessonIndex<lessons.length-1)lessonIndex++;else learningPage='practice';paintLearning();}if(target.hasAttribute('data-learning-retry'))refreshLearning();if(target.dataset.exerciseDone){target.disabled=true;learningData=await api('/api/learning',{exercise_id:target.dataset.exerciseDone});paintLearning();}if(target.dataset.practiceOpen){if(target.dataset.practiceOpen==='terminal'){document.querySelector('a[href="#terminal"]').click();}else{document.querySelector(`[data-tab="${target.dataset.practiceOpen}"]`).click();$('activity').scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth'});}}if(target.hasAttribute('data-load-reviews'))loadLearningReviews();if(target.dataset.reviewIndex!==undefined)openTradeReview($('learning-reviews')._report.closed[Number(target.dataset.reviewIndex)]);if(target.dataset.reportReview!==undefined&&reportData)openTradeReview(reportData.closed[Number(target.dataset.reportReview)]);if(target.hasAttribute('data-close-review'))$('trade-review-dialog').close();if(target.hasAttribute('data-review-chart')){const trade=currentReviewTrade;$('trade-review-dialog').close();if(typeof window.showTradeOnChart==='function')window.showTradeOnChart(trade);else toast('Chart markers are unavailable right now.');$('terminal').scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth'});}if(target.hasAttribute('data-review-journal')){$('trade-review-dialog').close();document.querySelector('[data-tab="journal"]').click();$('activity').scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth'});$('journal-text').focus();}}catch(e){toast(e.message);target.disabled=false;}});
document.addEventListener('submit',async event=>{if(event.target.id==='lesson-quiz'){event.preventDefault();const l=lessons[lessonIndex],answer=Number(new FormData(event.target).get('lesson-answer'));if(answer!==l.correct){$('quiz-feedback').textContent='Not quite. '+l.explain;return;}const button=event.submitter;button.disabled=true;try{learningData=await api('/api/learning',{lesson_id:l.id});$('quiz-feedback').textContent='✓ Completed. '+l.explain;document.querySelector('.learning-progress strong').textContent=learningData.completed.length+' / '+lessons.length;document.querySelector('.learning-progress progress').value=learningData.completed.length;document.querySelector(`[data-lesson="${lessonIndex}"] span`).textContent='✓ Completed';}catch(e){$('quiz-feedback').textContent=e.message;}finally{button.disabled=false;}}if(event.target.id==='trade-plan-form'){event.preventDefault();const data=Object.fromEntries(new FormData(event.target));data.max_loss=Number(data.max_loss);data.confidence=Number(data.confidence);const button=event.submitter;button.disabled=true;try{await api('/api/trade-plan',data);learningData=await api('/api/learning');paintLearning();toast('Trade plan saved locally. No order was placed.');}catch(e){$('plan-feedback').textContent=e.message;button.disabled=false;}}});

document.addEventListener('input',event=>{if(event.target.closest('.fno-lab'))paintFnoPayoff();});
document.addEventListener('change',event=>{if(event.target.closest('.fno-lab'))paintFnoPayoff();});
